  "id": "DrugBank_DB09064 - MONDO_7759",
  "verbalization": "Ciprofibrate, a fibrates drug, is implicated in the pathogenesis of hyperlipidemia through its ability to modulate lipid metabolism. The evidence suggests that Ciprofibrate may be linked to hyperlipidemia through a chain of indications: it is associated with hypertriglyceridemia, which in turn is linked to fenofibrate, a known activator of peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor (PPAR-α). Fenofibrate, in turn, is linked to hyperlipidemia, familial combined hyperlipidemia, and LPL-related hyperlipidemia. This suggests a potential mechanistic pathway where Ciprofibrate may influence lipid metabolism via PPAR-α activation, leading to the development of hyperlipidemia. The evidence is supported by a direct indication chain from Ciprofibrate to the target condition, with multiple pathways reinforcing the link. The observed scores indicate a moderate level of confidence in this relationship, though further validation would be needed to confirm the exact mechanism and clinical relevance.",
